    {
      "id": "js-201-let-const",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: let-and-const-vs-var\n//@ description: let and const vs var\n// --- let and const vs var ---\n// WHY: 'var' is function-scoped and hoisted to the top of its function,\n// which causes surprising bugs. 'let' and 'const' are BLOCK-scoped -\n// a block being anything between { and }, including an if or a for body.\n\n// var hoisting demo - this does NOT throw, it prints undefined\nvar hoistDemo = function() {\n    // Hoisting means the DECLARATION 'var x' is moved to the top of the function\n    // before it runs, while the assignment stays where you wrote it. So x already\n    // exists on this line, but holds nothing yet.\n    console.log('x before declaration:', x);  // undefined (hoisted, not ReferenceError)\n    var x = 5;\n    console.log('x after declaration:', x);   // 5\n};\nhoistDemo();\n\n// let: block-scoped, not hoisted in a useful way\n// A 'let' is also hoisted, but sits in the \"temporal dead zone\" until its line\n// executes - touching it early throws a ReferenceError instead of quietly\n// handing you undefined. That failure is the feature: it catches the bug early.\nconst blockScopeDemo = function() {\n    let score = 100;\n    if (true) {\n        // A second binding named 'score' that SHADOWS the outer one. With 'var'\n        // there would be only one variable and the outer value would be clobbered.\n        let score = 999;   // different 'score' - only lives inside this if-block\n        console.log('inside block:', score);   // 999\n    }\n    console.log('outside block:', score);  // 100 - unchanged\n};\nblockScopeDemo();\n\n// const: must be initialized, cannot be reassigned (but objects/arrays are still mutable!)\nconst PI = 3.14159;\n// PI = 3;  // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable\n\n// This is the point people trip over: const protects the BINDING (which object\n// the name points at), not the object's contents.\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\nuser.age = 31;           // OK - we're mutating the object, not reassigning the variable\n// user = {};            // TypeError - this would repoint the binding\nconsole.log('user.age after mutation:', user.age);\n\n// Rule of thumb: use const by default, let when you need to reassign, never var.\n//\n// One Arima-specific caveat: a notebook cell has to be re-runnable, and a\n// top-level const cannot be redeclared in a live session. So Arima treats a\n// const or let written at the START of a cell as a var. The rule above is the\n// real JavaScript rule and holds everywhere that matters - inside functions,\n// blocks and modules - but at cell top level a reassignment will not throw.\nconsole.log('PI:', PI);",
      "output": "",
      "returnValue": null,
      "executed": false,
      "executionCount": null,
